## Important

**0.4.0 has no image.** The npm packages are published, but the image build stopped. Self-hosters must use `ghcr.io/micacms/mica:0.4.1`. The local editor needs no change.

## Changed

A security review of the self-hosted Mica led to many fixes. The project also moved to the `micacms` organization, and the image moved with it.

## Breaking

**The image is `ghcr.io/micacms/mica`.** Change `image:` in your compose file. The old name, `ghcr.io/amuedespacher/micacms`, gets no new versions.

**Production needs an `https://` `MICA_PUBLIC_URL`.** With `NODE_ENV=production`, Mica does not start when the address is missing. It also does not start when the address is `http://`, unless it is on loopback. Put Mica behind a proxy that ends TLS.

**Editors sign in once more.** Over https, the session and CSRF cookies have new names, with the prefix `__Host-`.

**`/health` checks only the process.** It does no I/O. A provider outage no longer restarts the container. The new `/ready` checks the database and the repository. Point a monitor at `/ready` if it must see a repository outage.

**SVG is refused.** Mica does not accept SVG uploads and does not show SVG files, because an SVG can carry a script. The website build is not affected. Use PNG or WebP.

**`within()` from `@micacms/core/node` refuses the content root.** Call it with `{ allowRoot: true }` if you need the root.

## Security

- A content path can no longer leave the content root. Before, a signed-in editor could read any file in the repository through the media file route.
- A crafted JXL, HEIF or ICNS upload can no longer block the server.
- Every response has a Content-Security-Policy. The editor loads its font from Mica.
- Media files are served in a sandbox. Files that are not images are sent as downloads.
- The sign-in can no longer send a browser to another site.
- Health checks no longer use the API quota of the repository token. They no longer show the repository or the uptime.
- Over https, every response has `Strict-Transport-Security`.
- Mica refuses an oversized request before it reads it into memory.
- With `TRUST_PROXY=true`, a client can no longer choose its own address to avoid the sign-in rate limit.
- A refused sign-in no longer puts an email address in the URL.
- The sign-in cookie is signed with a key derived from `SESSION_SECRET`.
- The image is signed, with an SBOM and build provenance, and CI scans it. The workflows have read-only permissions and pinned actions. These parts are first available in 0.4.1.
